"idp_source":"gtwo_kml_202611200552","idp_filedate":1795153948000,"nhcTitle":"Offshore of the Southeast U.S. Coast","discussion":"Surface observations indicate that a broad low pressure area has  formed over southeastern Florida, the northwestern Bahamas, and the  adjacent waters. The low is currently producing disorganized showers  and thunderstorms extending from the central Florida peninsula  east-southeastward over the Atlantic. This system could acquire  subtropical or tropical characteristics while it moves generally  northward to north-northeastward near or just offshore of the  southeastern United States coast through the weekend.  1. Regardless of subtropical or tropical development, the system is  expected to produce strong, gusty winds, dangerous surf, rip  currents and coastal flooding along portions of the southeast and  mid-Atlantic U.S. coasts into early next week. In addition, heavy  rainfall and the threat of flash flooding will continue across  portions of the Florida peninsula today, potentially spreading  northward toward coastal portions of the Carolinas and the  mid-Atlantic over the weekend. Interests along the eastern coast of  the United States should continue to monitor the progress of this  system. Additional information on this system, including Gale  Warnings, can be found in High Seas Forecasts issued by the National  Weather Service, as well as products issued by your local National  Weather Service forecast office. \n Formation chance through 48 hours...low...30 percent. \n Formation chance through 7 days...medium...50 percent.","disturbance":1}}]}